<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sack of Constantinople (1204)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204 was the capture and plunder of the Byzantine capital by the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The crusaders established a Latin Empire in the city, and the Byzantine state fragmented into successor states. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> treat the sack as a blow from which the empire never fully recovered, even after Constantinople was retaken in 1261.</p></div>
